## Cream skimming

::: {.notes}
Here R and U point in opposite directions. In the real programs the efficiency difference was small either way, so the authors say any objection has to be about equity.
:::

**Notation:** reinforcement learning ($S$, $A$, $R$, $\pi$, $U$).

Send 100 who worked last year: $R$ = 70%, and $U$ = 6 more people in work. Send 100 who did not: $R$ = 40%, and $U$ = 26 more.

Choosing people who would have found work anyway is called cream skimming. US job-training centers funded under the Job Training Partnership Act were judged partly on employment rates. Economists who studied them found that cream skimming changed the programs' efficiency little, in either direction, and concluded that "opposition to cream skimming must come on equity grounds."

::: {.source}
Heckman, Heinrich and Smith (2002), "The Performance of Performance Standards," NBER Working Paper 9002, abstract and section VIII. They also found the short-run measures "weakly, and sometimes perversely, related to long run impacts."
:::

## Utilitarianism with a reward

::: {.notes}
Each difficulty of utilitarianism from part 1 is a choice someone makes when building the agent. The robot and the center are both in the first row.
